[[Presidential elections in France|Presidential elections]] were held in [[France]] on 21 and 22 April 2007 to elect the successor to [[Jacques Chirac]] as [[President of the French Republic|president]] of France (and ''ex officio'' [[Co-Prince of Andorra]]) for a five-year term. As no candidate received a majority of the vote, a second round was held on 5 and 6 May 2007 between the two leading candidates, [[Nicolas Sarkozy]] and [[Ségolène Royal]]. Sarkozy was elected with 53% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Sarkozy wins the race |url=https://www.economist.com/certain-ideas-of-europe/2007/05/06/sarkozy-wins-the-race |access-date=2025-08-04 |work=The Economist |issn=0013-0613}}</ref>

Sarkozy and Royal both represented a generational change. Both main candidates were born after World War II,<ref>{{cite news | agency=Associated Press | title = Voting begins in presidential election in France |work=International Herald Tribune | date = 22 April 2007 | url = http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/04/22/europe/EU-POL-France-Election.php | access-date = 29 April 2007}} "Either way, France will get its first president born after World War II, since both Royal and Sarkozy are in their fifties."</ref> along with the first to have seen adulthood under the [[French Fifth Republic|Fifth Republic]], and the first not to have been in politics under [[Charles de Gaulle]]. In addition, Royal was the first woman in France's history to reach the second round in a presidential election.

The election result has been interpreted as an example of [[center squeeze]], a kind of [[spoiler effect]] common to the [[plurality-rule family]] of voting rules, since Sarkozy, a conservative, and Royal, a socialist, eliminated moderate liberal [[François Bayrou]] in the first round, despite polls showing a majority of voters preferred Bayrou in a one-on-one match with either of his opponents.<ref name=":4">{{cite journal |last1=Keskin |first1=Umut |last2=Sanver |first2=M. Remzi |last3=Tosunlu |first3=H. Berkay |date=August 2022 |title=Monotonicity violations under plurality with a runoff: the case of French presidential elections |url=https://hal.science/hal-03413280/document |journal=Social Choice and Welfare |volume=59 |issue=2 |pages=305–333 |doi=10.1007/s00355-022-01397-4}}</ref><ref>{{Citation |last1=Balinski |first1=Michel |chapter=Election by Majority Judgment: Experimental Evidence |date=2011 |title=In Situ and Laboratory Experiments on Electoral Law Reform: French Presidential Elections |pages=13–54 |editor-last=Dolez |editor-first=Bernard |chapter-url=https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4419-7539-3_2 |access-date=2024-10-31 |place=New York, NY |publisher=Springer |language=en |doi=10.1007/978-1-4419-7539-3_2 |isbn=978-1-4419-7539-3 |last2=Laraki |first2=Rida |editor2-last=Grofman |editor2-first=Bernard |editor3-last=Laurent |editor3-first=Annie|chapter-url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Balinski |first1=Michel |last2=Laraki |first2=Rida |date=2020-03-01 |title=Majority judgment vs. majority rule |journal=Social Choice and Welfare |language=en |volume=54 |issue=2 |pages=429–461 |doi=10.1007/s00355-019-01200-x |issn=1432-217X |doi-access=free}}</ref>{{Not in body|date=July 2025}}

Sarkozy ran for [[2012 French presidential election|re-election]] in 2012, but was defeated by Royal's lifelong partner at the time, [[François Hollande]].<ref>Ministère de l'Interieur: [http://elections.interieur.gouv.fr/PR2012/FE.html Élection Présidentielle 2012, Résultats 2nd tour]</ref> Since the death of [[Lionel Jospin]] in 2026, it is the earliest French presidential election in which either of the second round candidates is still alive.

==Schedule==
*22 February 2007: The decree convoking the election was published in the {{lang|fr|[[Journal officiel de la République française]]}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/WAspad/UnTexteDeJorf?numjo=INTX0700024D |title=Decree n°2007-227 of 21&nbsp;February 2007 |language= fr |publisher=Legifrance.gouv.fr |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref>
*16 March 2007 – 18:00 (16:00 [[UTC]]): Deadline for candidates to have obtained the 500 sponsors from elected officials in at least 30 different [[Departments of France|departments]] or overseas territories which are required to run for president.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.elysee.fr/elysee/anglais/the_president/his_function/6_questions_about_the_president/6_questions_about_the_president.20030.html |title=6 questions about the president |publisher=Elysee.fr |access-date=13 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071006032333/http://www.elysee.fr/elysee/anglais/the_president/his_function/6_questions_about_the_president/6_questions_about_the_president.20030.html |archive-date=6 October 2007  }}</ref>
*19 March 2007 – 17:30 (15:30 UTC): Official candidate list was announced by the [[Constitutional Council of France|Constitutional Council]]: 12 candidates.<ref name="cc">{{cite web|url=http://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/decision/2007/20070319/index.htm |title=Decision of 19 March 2007, of the Constitutional Council |publisher=Conseil-constitutionnel.fr |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref>
*9 April 2007: Official campaign started.
*20 April 2007: Official campaign ended.
*21 April 2007: '''First round of voting''' started in [[Saint Pierre and Miquelon]] at 8&nbsp;am local time (10:00 UTC) and subsequently took place in [[Guadeloupe]], Martinique, [[French Guiana]], [[French Polynesia]], and in voting offices in [[embassy|embassies]] and consulates in the Americas.
*22 April 2007: '''First round of voting''' took place in [[Wallis and Futuna]], [[New Caledonia]], Réunion, [[Mayotte]], [[Metropolitan France]], and in voting offices in embassies and consulates in Oceania, Asia, Africa and Europe – the last polling stations closed in the large cities of Metropolitan France at 8&nbsp;pm local time (18:00 UTC) and publication of the first exit polls were allowed immediately after they closed.
*25 April 2007: Official results of the first round announced.
*27 April 2007: Official candidate list for second round announced.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/dossier/presidentielles/2007/documents/tour2/20070426.htm |last=Le Conseil Constitutionnel |title=Décision du 26 avril 2007 |date=26 April 2007 |access-date=23 March 2018 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070501103202/http://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/dossier/presidentielles/2007/documents/tour2/20070426.htm |archive-date=1 May 2007 }}</ref>
*2 May 2007 – 21:00 (19:00 UTC): Nationally televised [[French presidential debates|debate]] between the two candidates.
*5 May 2007: '''Second round of voting''' started in [[Saint Pierre and Miquelon]] at 8&nbsp;am local time (10:00 UTC) and subsequently took place in [[Guadeloupe]], Martinique, [[French Guiana]], [[French Polynesia]], and in voting offices in [[embassy|embassies]] and consulates in the Americas.
*6 May 2007: '''Second round of voting''' took place in [[Wallis and Futuna]], [[New Caledonia]], Réunion, [[Mayotte]], [[Metropolitan France]], and in voting offices in embassies and consulates in Oceania, Asia, Africa and Europe – the last polling stations closed in the large cities of Metropolitan France at 8&nbsp;pm local time (18:00 UTC) and publication of the first exit polls were allowed immediately after they closed.
*10 May 2007: Official results of the second round announced.
*16 May 2007 – Midnight (22:00 UTC):  Expiration of the term of president [[Jacques Chirac]].

==Electoral system==
[[File:Election MG 3455.JPG|thumb|Transparent ballot boxes have been required since 1988.]]
[[File:IVotronic img 3452.jpg|thumb|upright|Some French cities used [[voting machine]]s.]]

For the first time in a presidential election, [[electronic voting]] was introduced in some areas, having been authorised in 2004. They were introduced in only 82 of 36,000 [[French communes|voting districts]], and were criticised by a number of people, both on the left and on the right.<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/2007/04/17/business/evote.php France to choose president with help of electronic voting], ''[[International Herald Tribune]]'', 17 April 2007 {{in lang|en}}</ref> A [[petition]] against them has also been made (see [[n:Electronic voting disputed in France|Wikinews:Electronic voting disputed in France]]).

Using the three colours of the national flag (blue, white, and red) on electoral advertisements or partisan documentation was prohibited by electoral regulations.<ref>Electoral code, [http://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/WAspad/UnArticleDeCode?commun=&code=CELECTOM.rcv&art=R27 article R27]: "Posters and flyers, having an electoral goal or character, and including some combination of the three colours blue, white, and red (except the reproduction of an emblem of a party or political group), are prohibited"</ref> Ségolène Royal contended that the book ''Ensemble'' ("together") published by Nicolas Sarkozy, whose cover is blue, white and red,<ref>Nicolas Sarkozy, ''Ensemble'', Xo, 3 April 2007, {{ISBN|2-84563-345-9}}</ref> was effectively an electoral partisan documentation and should have been covered by this prohibition.<ref>''[https://web.archive.org/web/20070407110539/http://www.lemonde.fr/web/article/0,1-0@2-823448,36-891681,0.html M. Sarkozy et Mme Royal se rendent coup pour coup]'', [[Le Monde]]</ref>

==Candidates==
===Requirements===
:''Reference: [https://web.archive.org/web/20070126071151/http://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/dossier/presidentielles/2007/faq/p01.htm Constitutional Council, FAQ]''
{{further|President of the French Republic}}
The requirements for being [[nomination rules|successfully nominated as a candidate]] are defined by the organic law of 6 November 1962.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/dossier/presidentielles/2007/documents/lopdr62.htm |last=Conseil Constitutionnel |title=Loi N° 16-1292 du 6 novembre 1962 |date= 6 November 1962 |access-date=23 March 2018 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070204104747/http://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/dossier/presidentielles/2007/documents/lopdr62.htm |archive-date=4 February 2007 }}</ref>

All candidates had to be of French nationality and at least 23 years old (the same requirement as for the candidates to the [[National Assembly of France|National Assembly]]){{citation needed|date=March 2026}}.

Candidates must obtain signatures from 500 elected officials (mayors, members of Parliament, elected representatives) supporting their candidacy.  These signatures from elected officials (informally known in French as ''parrainages'', but legally known as "presentations") must be from at least 30 [[Departments of France|departments]] or overseas territories, and no more than 10 percent can be from any individual department. A presentation from an elected official does not imply the official supports the policies of the candidate, but rather that this official considers the candidate to be a serious candidate.

Candidates must also submit a statement with details of their personal assets.

The [[Constitutional Council of France|Constitutional Council]] published the official candidate list on 20 March 2007. The candidates were listed in a [[randomisation|randomised]] order. This order was used for the official campaign: thus, posters for Olivier Besancenot were to always be displayed on the No. 1 board, those for Marie-George Buffet on the No. 2 board, etc., regardless of where in France the boards were located.

There were 12 candidates for the 2007 election.<ref name="cc" />

===Leading candidates===
Four candidates consistently registered over 10% in the opinion polls and were regarded as having a reasonable chance of reaching the second round.<ref>[http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/nationworld/bal-te.france09mar09,0,2949377.story?coll=bal-nationworld-headlines 3-way presidential race building in France]{{dead link|date=August 2026|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>
* [[Nicolas Sarkozy]] was nominated by the [[Union for a Popular Movement]] on 14 January 2007. He was the leader of the UMP and was [[Minister of the Interior (France)|Interior Minister]] until stepping down to focus on his campaign on 26 March 2007.
* [[Ségolène Royal]] was selected by the [[French Socialist Party|Socialist Party]] on [[2006 French Socialist Party presidential primary|17 November 2006]] to be the party's candidate for the election. She won 60.6% of the votes in a ballot of party members to choose their candidate, against 20.8% for [[Dominique Strauss-Kahn]] and 18.5% for [[Laurent Fabius]]. She was the first woman to represent a major French party in a presidential contest.
* [[François Bayrou]] was nominated by the [[centrism|centrist]] [[Union for French Democracy]] (UDF) on 2 December 2006.
*[[Jean-Marie Le Pen]] ran for the [[National Front (France)|National Front]], a far-right party which promotes policies of strong law enforcement, economic [[protectionism]] and strong measures to control immigration.  As during previous presidential campaigns, Le Pen raised the question of whether he would be able to obtain the necessary 500 signatures on a number of occasions, which he claims is the result of pressure placed on elected officials by the major parties to support their own candidate (he has often claimed, during past elections, that "political elites" have sabotaged his campaigns); however, on 14 March 2007 his party said that he had obtained the necessary signatures.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/6449711.stm |title=France's Le Pen 'to run' in poll, BBC |work=BBC News  |date=15 March 2007 |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref>

