===Confirmed non-candidates===
*President [[Jacques Chirac]] announced on 11 March 2007 that he would not be standing for another term as president.  It had been rumoured that President Chirac was considering running for a third term, following statements he made at the beginning of 2007, including his New Year's Address on 31 December 2006, and subsequent speeches which contained robust comments on international policy and detailed national policy proposals with a suggested five-year timetable.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/6234759.stm |work=BBC News  | title=Chirac renews attack on Iraq war | date=5 January 2007 | access-date=2 May 2010}}</ref> In March, Chirac announced his support for Sarkozy.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/6474573.stm |title=Chirac Backs Sarkozy in Poll Race |work=BBC News  |date=21 March 2007 |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref> There was no provision at the time in the [[Constitution of France|Constitution of 1958]] specifying a limited number of terms, though a third term would have been unprecedented under the [[French Fifth Republic|Fifth Republic]].
*[[Christine Boutin]] announced that she would not be a candidate for the election and pledged her support for [[Nicolas Sarkozy]] (source: [[France 2]] news, 2 December 2006).
*[[Rachid Kaci]], member of the UMP and President of the group Free Right (la Droite Libre), announced his withdrawal as candidate and also pledged to support Nicolas Sarkozy on 21 December 2006 during a UMP public Forum.
*MRC chairman [[Jean-Pierre Chevènement]] announced on 10 December 2006 that he would not be running, and that his movement would back Ségolène Royal in return for an electoral agreement in the 2007 general election.
*Candidate for the [[Left Radical Party|Radical Party]], [[Christiane Taubira]] in the 2002 election, confirmed that she would not be running following an electoral agreement between her party and the Socialist Party.  The Left Radicals in return supported Ségolène Royal.
*[[Nicolas Hulot]], television presenter and environmental activist, was widely considered to be a possible candidate following the positive media and public reaction to his recent book and Environmental Charter.  On 3 January 2007 ''[[Le Figaro]]'' newspaper reported that supporters of Hulot had begun gathering signatures to mount a campaign and a website,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://nicolashulot2007.org/ |title = Soutien à la candidature de Nicolas Hulot en 2007  |access-date=3 January 2007 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070216120605/http://nicolashulot2007.org/ |archive-date=16 February 2007  }}</ref> was created to generate support. On 22 January he announced that he would not be a candidate.
*[[Corinne Lepage]], environmentalist politician and activist, withdrew her candidacy in favour of Bayrou on 10 March 2007.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.lemonde.fr/web/depeches/0,14-0,39-30072777@7-37,0.html |title=Les Dépêches |publisher=Le Monde.fr |access-date=13 June 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070314222339/http://www.lemonde.fr/web/depeches/0,14-0,39-30072777@7-37,0.html |archive-date=14 March 2007 |url-status=dead  }}</ref>
* [[Roland Castro]], architect and "utopian left" activist, withdrew his candidacy on 12 March 2007.
* [[Édouard Fillias]]: [[Alternative Libérale]], a new French libertarian party, withdrew his candidacy on 13 March 2007 in favour of Bayrou.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.lemonde.fr/web/depeches/0,14-0,39-30104084@7-354,0.html |title=Les Dépêches |publisher=Le Monde.fr |access-date=13 June 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070324033325/http://www.lemonde.fr/web/depeches/0,14-0,39-30104084@7-354,0.html |archive-date=24 March 2007 |url-status=dead  }}</ref>
* [[Antoine Waechter]]: [[Independent Ecological Movement]], withdrew his candidacy on 14 March 2007

