==Results==
The first round saw a very high turnout of 83.8% – 36.7&nbsp;million of the 44.5&nbsp;million [[constituency|electorate]] voted from a [[demographics of France|population]] of 64.1&nbsp;million (not including French people living abroad).<ref>Including children and non-registered people (citizens and non-citizens)</ref><ref name=Libe>[http://www.liberation.fr/actualite/politiques/elections2007/ Elections 2007] on the website of ''[[Libération]]'' {{in lang|fr}} {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070420151327/http://www.liberation.fr/actualite/politiques/elections2007/ |date=20 April 2007 }}</ref><ref>[http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/6582541.stm France left with clear choice], ''[[BBC]]'', 23 April 2007 {{in lang|en}}</ref> The results of that round saw Sarkozy and Royal qualify for the second round with Sarkozy getting 31% and Royal 26%. [[François Bayrou]] came third (19%) and [[Jean-Marie Le Pen]] fourth (10%), unlike [[2002 French presidential election|in 2002]] when Le Pen got a surprising 16.9% and qualified for the second round.<ref name=MondeResults>[http://www.lemonde.fr/web/vi/0,47-0@2-823448,54-899922@51-899913,0.html Results] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070424013137/http://www.lemonde.fr/web/vi/0,47-0@2-823448,54-899922@51-899913,0.html |date=24 April 2007 }} from ''[[Le Monde]]'' {{in lang|fr}}</ref>

Immediately after the first round's results were made official, four defeated left-wing candidates – [[José Bové]], [[Marie-George Buffet]], [[Arlette Laguiller]] and [[Dominique Voynet]] – urged their supporters to vote for Royal.<ref>[http://www.news24.com/News24/World/News/0,,2-10-1462_2102972,00.html French left rallies behind Royal: World: News: News24] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070525210400/http://www.news24.com/News24/World/News/0,,2-10-1462_2102972,00.html |date=25 May 2007 }}</ref> This was the first time since 1981 that Laguiller had endorsed the Socialist Party's candidate.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.asiantribune.com/index.php?q=node/5436 |title=French presidential election: Sarkozy and Royal to compete in second round |publisher=Asian Tribune |date=23 April 2007 |access-date=13 June 2010 |archive-date=24 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191224222024/http://www.asiantribune.com/index.php?q=node/5436 |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Olivier Besancenot]] called his supporters to vote against Sarkozy.<ref>[http://besancenot2007.org/spip.php?rubrique19 Déclaration d’Olivier Besancenot] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070425224620/http://besancenot2007.org/spip.php?rubrique19 |date=25 April 2007 }} {{in lang|fr}}</ref> [[Frédéric Nihous]] and [[Gérard Schivardi]] never officially supported either Royal or Sarkozy. [[Philippe de Villiers]] called for a vote for Sarkozy.<ref>[http://www.lemonde.fr/web/depeches/0,14-0,39-30659299@7-354,0.html Philippe de Villiers appelle à voter pour Nicolas Sarkozy]{{Dead link|date=October 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, ''[[Le Monde]]'', 25 April 2007 {{in lang|fr}}</ref> Le Pen told his voters to "abstain massively" in the second round.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://news.monstersandcritics.com/europe/news/article_1298683.php/Le_Pen_tells_his_supporters_to_abstain_in_French_election |title=Le Pen tells his supporters to abstain in French election |date=1 May 2007 |access-date=2 May 2007 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070505024629/http://news.monstersandcritics.com/europe/news/article_1298683.php/Le_Pen_tells_his_supporters_to_abstain_in_French_election |archive-date=5 May 2007  }}</ref>

On 25 April, Bayrou declared he would not support either candidate in the runoff,<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20070810091337/http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?f=%2Fn%2Fa%2F2007%2F04%2F25%2Finternational%2Fi065134D16.DTL&type=politics Bayrou Refuses to Endorse Candidate], ''[[Sfgate]]'', 25 April 2007 {{in lang|en}}</ref> and announced he would form a new political party called the [[Democratic Movement (France)|Democratic Movement]].  He criticised both major candidates, and offered to debate them.  Royal agreed to hold a televised debate, while Sarkozy offered to have a private discussion but not a televised [[French presidential debates|debate]].<ref>[http://www.lemonde.fr/web/video/0,47-0@2-823448,54-901898,0.html François Bayrou refuse de donner une consigne de vote] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070428014902/http://www.lemonde.fr/web/video/0,47-0@2-823448,54-901898,0.html |date=28 April 2007 }}, (video), ''[[Le Monde]]'', 25 March 2007 {{in lang|fr}}</ref>

By around 18:15 local time on 6 May, Belgian and Swiss news sources such as ''[[Le Soir]]'',<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lesoir.be/ |title=actu |publisher=lesoir.be |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref> [[RTBF]],<ref>{{cite web |url=http://evenement.rtbf.be/ |title=RTBF – EVENEMENT – Elections fédérales 10 juin 2007 |publisher=Evenement |access-date=13 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090312064929/http://evenement.rtbf.be/ |archive-date=12 March 2009  }}</ref> '' [[La Libre Belgique]]''<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lalibre.be/ |title=Comprendre c'est déjà agir |publisher=Lalibre.be |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref> and ''[[Tribune de Genève|La Tribune de Genève]]''<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tdg.ch/ |title=Tribune de Genève |publisher=Tdg.ch |access-date=13 June 2010}}</ref> had announced Nicolas Sarkozy as the winner of the second round, citing preliminary exit poll data. The final CSA estimate showed him winning with 53% of the votes cast. Royal conceded defeat to Sarkozy that evening.<ref>[http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/6630797.stm Sarkozy takes French presidency], ''[[BBC News]]'', 6 May 2007</ref>

